   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to establish a commission to be known as the \"New York seawall study\ncommission\"; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7141",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7141-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 32,
       "fullName": "Luis R. Sepúlveda",
       "imgName": "493_Luis_Sepulveda.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1142,
       "sessionMemberId": 1836,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "SEPULVEDA"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "New York City",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7141\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to establish a commission to be known as the \"New York seawall\nstudy commission\"; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nexpiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo set up a commission to study the feasibility, costs, impacts, and\nbest locations for construction of a seawall around the city of New\nYork's coastline.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe Commission shall consist of members who shall be appointed as\nfollows:\n\nThe Commissioner of Environmental Conservation or his/her designee; The\nSecretary of State or his/her designee;\n\n3 members appointed by the Governor at least 1 of whom shall have exper-\ntise in environmental justice issues;\n\n2 members to be appointed by the Temporary President of the Senate; 2\nmembers to be appointed by the Speaker of the Assembly;\n\n1 member to be appointed by the Minority Leader of the Senate; 1 member\nto be appointed by the Minority leader of the Assembly; 2 members to be\nappointed by the Mayor of New York City; 1 member to be appointed by the\nCounty Executive of. Nassau; 1 member to be appointed by the County\nExecutive of Suffolk; and, 1 member to be appointed by the County Execu-\ntive of Westchester Members should include individuals with expertise in\none or more of the following areas: climatology; hydrology; environ-\nmental; aquaculture; flood risk management; environmental justice;\necological restoration and management; or coastal ocean science; and\nengineering.\n\nBy December 31, 2023 the Commission shall deliver to the Governor and\nLegislature a report to include:\n\nCoastal areas at greatest risk from sea level rise; Areas at risk from\nstorm surge based on current sea levels; Areas that are more likely to\nbe at risk from coastal hazards, including from extreme weather events,\nwith projections extending through year 2100; Locations for construction\nof a seawall including -any communities to he at risk from such\nconstruction; Feasibility, costs and locations of nature-based solutions\nand on shore protection measures; Costs including impacts to vulnerable\npopulations and natural resources; and, Recommendations on state and\nlocal regulatory and/or statutory alterations\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn just the last few years, we have seen one horrific storm after anoth-\ner. These once in a lifetime storms are becoming more and more common.\nNew York City must be prepared for the next Hurricane Sandy.  A seawall\nis one idea that could possibly save lives in the next super-storm, as\nwell as protect vital infrastructure that could cost billions to repair.\nThis piece of legislation would create a commission to study the New\nYork City coastline to determine the feasibility of a seawall. In 1900,\na Category 4 hurricane, the \"Great Storm\", struck Galveston, Texas kill-\ning 6,000 to 12,000 individuals.  It was the deadliest natural disaster\nin U.S history. In 1915, a similar storm in strength struck Galveston,\nbut the seawall proved invaluable for protection of-the city and its\nresidents. A seawall as seen in Galveston, can help save lives and\nprotect infrastructure. A study of how such a seawall can be of vital\nservice to the New York City coast makes perfect sense.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S.8841 - Referred to Rules.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
